---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RandomStringGenerator from '../../components/RandomStringGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Random String Generator" description="Generate random strings for tokens, test data, or IDs — custom length and character set, cryptographically secure.">
  <RandomStringGenerator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Common use cases</h2>
  <p>API keys for local testing, unique identifiers, seed values, or placeholder data — this generates cryptographically random strings from your chosen character set.</p>

  <h2>Common Random String Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using this for real production secrets without understanding scope</strong> — while cryptographically random, always treat generated strings as test/placeholder unless integrated into a proper secret management flow.</li>
    <li><strong>Choosing too short a length</strong> for a security-sensitive use case, undermining the randomness benefit with insufficient total entropy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Random String Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Generating placeholder API keys or tokens for local development</li>
    <li>Creating unique test identifiers for automated testing</li>
    <li>Generating random seed values for data generation scripts</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Random String Generator FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this cryptographically secure?</strong> Yes — it uses your browser's <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, not <code>Math.random()</code>, making it suitable for security-sensitive test values, though production secrets should still be generated and managed server-side.</p>
  <p><strong>Can I exclude ambiguous characters like 0 and O?</strong> Choose a character set that excludes them if your use case involves humans reading or typing the string manually — for machine-only usage, the full set maximizes entropy.</p>
</ToolLayout>
